  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A thin metallic wire having cross sectional area of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> is used to make a ring of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. A positive charge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> is uniformly distributed over the ring, while another positive charge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo> </mo><mi>pC</mi></math> is kept at the centre of the ring. The tension in the ring is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>_______</mi><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>; provided that the ring does not get deformed (neglect the influence of gravity).</p><p>(Given, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>9</mn></msup></math> SI units)</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "3",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/ac5fbda3-2d09-40cf-ba2a-fc68f379037b.png\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 253px;\"/></p><p>The linear charge density of the ring is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>With reference to the above diagram, for the equilibrium condition of the ring, it can be written that</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mfrac><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>·</mo><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mfenced><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfenced><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> [As for very small angles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>sin</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>]</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mfenced><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>30</mn><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math></p>",
